A||||9||Getaway2k||Oil pan gasket?||||1||
Z||000000||Getaway2k||10-27-2000||08:07 PM||||I noticed a few small oil spots under my Fiero which led me to take a further look. It looks like oil is dripping from the pan plug and from a few bolt heads that are near the rear of the motor (close to the trans pan). I'm assuming that this is a leaking oil pan gasket as the entire oil pan is also slopped up and I was wondering how hard it is to change the gasket? It's an 86 GT with a v6. Any advice or opinions of what else it could be would be greatly appreciated.<P>Thanks-||||reg||1
Z||000001||sluppy123||10-27-2000||10:02 PM||||Getaway- Have a look at this thread.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000604-2-001450.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000604-2-001450.html</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>-Brian S. <BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle||||reg||3
Z||000002||Getaway2k||10-28-2000||09:12 AM||||Thanks! I knew that the Haynes manual made it sound too easy!||||reg||1
Z||000003||MCordray||10-28-2000||09:19 AM||||I have an 86 GT as well, and it's a messy job.  But, I was able to pull the pan without removing the exhaust springs.  Once you remove the right engine mount, and use a jack to support the engine, you might be able to just drop the pan.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Getaway2k||10-28-2000||08:10 PM||||What specific spot are you placing the floor jack under to lift the motor and remove the mount (are you leaving the mount attached on top and just removing the 2 bottom bolt?)? Also, how high do you have to go with the motor to get the pan out? Do I need to remove or loosen the dog bone? I plan on dropping the exhaust and removing the starter (how hard is it to remove the starter, 2 easy access bolts, right?!)? The car is currently on 4 jackstands and has an aftrmarket rear sway bar. I'm curious as to how the suspension will move once I remove the sway bar (while it's on the jackstands). Any additional info/advice that you guys can give me would be greatly appreciated. <P>Thanks<p>[This message has been edited by Getaway2k (edited 10-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||MCordray||10-29-2000||09:20 AM||||I placed the floor jack underneath the tranny.  But, I used a small block of wood between the floor jack plate and the tranny, to provide some cushion.  And, yes, I only removed the bolts on the underside of the motor mount.  Basically, I'd remove the pan bolts, let it drop, then start messing around with the motor mount, floor jack, and anything else that seems to be in the way.  If you have an aftermarket rear sway, then that will probably have to come out too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||johnmac||10-29-2000||03:12 PM||||I am doing this exact job right now. What a lousy engineering job.<P>I had to remove the engine mount via a small hydraulic jack under the Alternator Bracket. Then I had to lower the 'Entire Exhaust system to get the pan out.<P>It is a rough job!<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000007||Getaway2k||10-29-2000||04:28 PM||||Thanks for all of the info everyone. Please keep it coming! Tips, idea's, anything to make the job easier!<P>Johnmac- if you could keep me posted on how it goes and what all you run into, I'd really appreciate it.<P>I'm going to start this job in a week or so. I've read where coolant lines etc.. have been snapped by jacking the car up to remove the pan. I am pretty leary about the whole job at this point!<P>Thanks again<P><p>[This message has been edited by Getaway2k (edited 10-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||10-29-2000||05:05 PM||||I got mine off without removing the motor mount. I notched the mount bracket with a torch so that the pan would clear it. I learned this trick from another Fiero I bought, I could never figure out why the mount had been cut until I had to remove the pan. I also had to remove some of the exhaust springs and unbolt the starter.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000009||johnmac||10-29-2000||09:09 PM||||To add more detail to removing the oil pan which I just done last night.<P>1) Remove 2 cover plates bu starter<P>2) Remove starter<P>3) Loosen Passenger side engine mount and bracket to alternator<P>4) Place small hydraulic jack on lower macpherson strut (Triangle member) and jack up alternator bracket slowly. At same time remove 2 lower nuts and lower bolt of engine mount. BE CAREFUL AS THIS IS A VERY FRAGILE LIFT - WATCH YOUR HANDS PLACEMENT.<P>5) When bracket raises up about 2 inches, remove engine mount on frame.<P>6) Swing engine mount bracket out of the way.<P>7) May want to do this as step 3 first.<BR>Remove rear exhaust springs brackets by muffler. Remove other exhaust piping restraining springs.<P>8) Slide out oil pan<P>9) Place engine mount on chassis back in <P>10) Place engine bracket back on mount<P>11) Lower Engine back on engine mount<P>12) Remove hydraulic jack.<P>- - - -<P>I have already cleaned out my pan. The sludge inside indicated that it was a long time before the pan was pulled - if ever. Now I know why! Fortunately no indication of metal filings!!<P>There is a antisplash plate built in. Painted it with high temperature resistant paint.<P>I threw away the old oil pan drain plug and installed a new one with a built in magnet.<P>Hope this helps.<P>John MacHaffie||||reg||1
